Title:
Demystifying AV/EDR Evasion

Type:
Conference Talk (Slide Deck)

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This slide deck breaks down practical AV vs EDR detection models and how common C2 payloads (Metasploit/Meterpreter and Cobalt Strike Beacon) actually execute in memory. It starts with basic shellcode loader mechanics (allocate, copy, execute) and why simple “encrypt the shellcode” only helps against static signatures, not runtime telemetry and memory scanning. A core case study demystifies Meterpreter “shellcode” as sRDI/reflective DLL loading, explaining why additional allocations and threads appear (ReflectiveLoader, DllMain) and how that impacts OPSEC and detections. It then pivots to “memory gymnastics”: why EDRs scrutinize unbacked/private executable pages and how module-backed execution (e.g., module stomping, Malleable C2 options like `module_x64` and `userwx`) can reduce suspicious memory indicators. The deck highlights newer approaches for more control over reflective loading (UDRL, BokuLoader, AceLoader), including IAT hooking and return-address spoofing, and notes an industry trend away from classic reflective loading. Useful for red team operators and implant/tooling developers who need to understand payload internals to tune tradecraft against modern telemetry.

Technical Focus:
- AV static vs dynamic vs cloud analysis; EDR telemetry concepts
- Shellcode loaders: VirtualAlloc/NtAllocateVirtualMemory, thread creation (CreateThread/NtCreateThreadEx)
- Reflective DLL Injection and sRDI (Meterpreter/Beacon internals, DOS header hotpatch/trampolines)
- Memory scanning triggers and post-callback detection (pipes, CLR/.NET inline execute-assembly)
- Module-backed vs private/unbacked executable memory; module stomping
- User-Defined Reflective Loader (UDRL), IAT hooking, return address spoofing

Use Cases:
- Reverse engineer Meterpreter/Beacon execution flow to explain “mystery” allocations/threads in debuggers (x64dbg)
- Build or modify shellcode loaders to reduce static signatures and improve runtime OPSEC
- Tune Cobalt Strike Malleable C2 (`module_x64`, `userwx`) to influence memory protections/backing
- Evaluate EDR detections around unbacked RX/RWX memory and test module-backed alternatives (e.g., Elastic)
- Prototype custom reflective loaders (UDRL/BokuLoader/AceLoader-style) for controlled import resolution and call stacks

Keywords:
AV, EDR, Metasploit, Meterpreter, Cobalt Strike, Beacon, shellcode loader, NtAllocateVirtualMemory, NtCreateThreadEx, reflective DLL injection, ReflectiveLoader, sRDI, DOS header hotpatch, trampoline, module stomping, module-backed memory, private RX/RWX, Malleable C2, AMSI, IAT hooking, return address spoofing, inlineExecute-Assembly, CLR loading, Elastic 8.12.2